.Dd October 14, 2026
.Dt BOOTD.HCL 5
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm bootd.hcl
.Nd bootd configuration file
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Xr bootd 8
reads its configuration from one file in HCL, HashiCorp's configuration
language: attributes are written
.Ar name No = Ar value ,
blocks
.Ar type Qo Ar label Qc Brq ... ,
strings are quoted, lists are written
.Li [\&"a\&", \&"b\&"]
and maps
.Li { key = \&"value\&" } .
Comments start with
.Li # ,
.Li //
or are enclosed in
.Li /* */ .
.Pp
The file describes the
.Ic network Ns s
.Xr bootd 8
is attached to, the
.Ic service Ns s
(protocols) it offers, and the
.Ic host Ns s
it serves, with the services each may use and the files it may load.
.Ic class Ns es
hold what several hosts share.
.Pp
.Nm bootd Cm check
reports errors and warnings with file and line.
.Sh TOP LEVEL
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Ic root No = Ar path
The directory relative paths of files, disks, exports and NIS maps are
relative to.
A relative
.Ic root
is relative to the directory of the configuration file, which is also
the default.
.It Ic log Brq ...
Logging:
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Ic level No = Ar string
.Cm debug ,
.Cm info
(the default),
.Cm warn
or
.Cm error .
.It Ic format No = Ar string
.Cm text
(the default) or
.Cm json .
.It Ic file No = Ar path
Where to log:
.Cm stderr
(the default),
.Cm stdout ,
or a file.
.It Ic services No = Ar map
The level for single services, e.g.\&
.Li { tftp = \&"debug\&" } .
.El
.It Ic resolve Brq ...
How addresses that a host does not give are looked up:
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Ic ethers No = Ar path
The
.Xr ethers 5
file for Ethernet addresses, by host name.
The default is
.Pa /etc/ethers ;
an empty string disables it.
.It Ic dns No = Ar bool
Whether to look up IP addresses with the system resolver, which includes
.Pa /etc/hosts .
The default is true.
.El
.El
.Sh NETWORKS
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Ic network Qo Ar name Qc Brq ...
An Ethernet segment.
.Xr bootd 8
has its own Ethernet and IP address on it and runs its own IP stack
there.
Frames are exchanged over UDP with
.Ic udp ,
or captured on an interface with
.Ic pcap .
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Ic address No = Ar address/prefix
bootd's IPv4 address and prefix length on the network, e.g.\&
.Li 192.168.1.250/24 .
Required.
.It Ic mac No = Ar address
bootd's Ethernet address on the network.
The default is a locally administered address derived from the network's
name.
.Cm interface
uses the address of the
.Ic pcap
interface, which Wi-Fi needs.
.It Ic udp No = Ar addr : Ns Ar port
The UDP address to exchange Ethernet frames on, one frame per datagram,
with
.Xr bootbridge 8 ,
simh or QEMU.
Addresses that send to it are learned as peers.
.It Ic peers No = Ar list
UDP addresses that always receive frames, and a keepalive every minute.
.It Ic l2tp Brq ...
Wrap the frames sent over
.Ic udp
in static L2TPv3, as
.Xr bootbridge 8 ,
Linux and QEMU speak it:
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Ic session No = Ar number
The session ID in both directions.
The default is 1.
.It Ic cookie No = Ar string
8 or 16 hex digits, the same in both directions, e.g.\&
.Li \&"0x0123456789abcdef\&" .
Datagrams without it are ignored.
.It Ic counter No = Ar bool
Whether frames carry the L2-specific sublayer with a sequence number.
The default is true.
.El
.It Ic pcap No = Ar interface
Capture on a network interface directly, instead of
.Ic udp .
This needs privileges.
.El
.El
.Sh SERVICES
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Ic service Qo Ar name Qc Brq ...
Enable a protocol.
Requests for a protocol without a
.Ic service
block are not answered.
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Ic networks No = Ar list
The networks the service is offered on.
The default is every network.
.El
.Pp
The other settings depend on the protocol:
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Ic service Qq rarp
Reverse ARP.
It answers with bootd's address on the network, and needs the host's
Ethernet and IP address.
No settings.
.It Ic service Qq rmp
HP Remote Maintenance Protocol, for HP 9000/300 and /400 boot ROMs.
Clients without a host entry are matched to a class by the machine type
they send.
The boot ROM lists the host's files in configuration order.
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Ic server_name No = Ar string
The name sent to clients asking for the server.
The default is the host name.
.El
.It Ic service Qq mop
DEC MOP Dump/Load, for the consoles of VAXen, DECstations and Alphas and
DEC terminal servers.
Loads only; dumps are not taken.
Clients are identified by Ethernet address.
bootd volunteers to load a program when the client may load it, and a file
named
.Pa NAME ,
.Pa NAME.SYS ,
.Pa name
or
.Pa name.sys
matches a request for
.Ar NAME .
A request without a name, the usual case, gets the host's first file.
Files are loaded as NetBSD's mopd does: ELF32 and a.out files from address 0
with their segments padded to their place in memory, and MOP images (with a
VMS image header) at the address in their header.
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Ic server_name No = Ar string
The server name sent at the end of a load, at most six characters.
The default is the host name, in capitals.
.El
.It Ic service Qq rpl
IBM Remote Program Load, for the boot ROMs of PC network cards.
Clients are identified by Ethernet address and get all the host's RPL
files, each at its
.Ic load
address, then start at the
.Ic execute
address.
.Pp
A file with
.Ic floppy
is a floppy image of 160, 180, 320 or 360 KB (or another size with a BIOS
parameter block, up to 447 KB) that boots as drive A:, e.g.\&
a DOS boot disk.
It is loaded to end at 576 KB, with a 1 KB loader below it that takes over
INT 13h for drive A: and starts the image's boot sector; DOS gets the
memory below the loader.
Writes to the disk stay in memory until the client restarts.
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Ic send_delay No = Ar milliseconds
The pause before each block: RPL sends the whole image without waiting
for the client.
The default is 10.
.El
.It Ic service Qq nd
Sun Network Disk, for diskless Sun-2s running SunOS 1 and 2: the boot
PROM reads public unit 0 before it knows its IP address, and the kernel
takes its root, swap and other units from the host's disks.
The host needs an Ethernet address and an IP address; clients learn
theirs from bootd's replies.
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Ic window No = Ar number
1 KB packets sent before waiting for the client.
The default is 6.
.It Ic send_delay No = Ar milliseconds
The pause before each packet, for clients that cannot keep up.
The default is 10.
.El
.It Ic service Qq tftp
TFTP, read only, with the blksize, tsize and timeout options.
Clients are identified by IP address.
A request for a path such as
.Pa /tftpboot/name
also matches a file named
.Pa name ,
and a request with an empty name, as 187Bug sends when no boot file name
is set, gets the host's first file.
Refusals of requests sent to a broadcast address are only logged, so that
another server can answer.
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Ic port No = Ar number
The default is 69.
.It Ic timeout No = Ar seconds
Before retransmitting, unless the client asks for another timeout.
The default is 2.
.It Ic retries No = Ar number
How often a packet is sent before giving up.
The default is 5.
.It Ic max_blksize No = Ar number
The largest blksize option accepted.
The default, 1468, keeps packets within one Ethernet frame.
.El
.It Ic service Qq bootparam
Sun bootparam, with a built-in portmapper.
.Li GETFILE Ar name
answers with the host's
.Ic export Qq Ar name ,
so bootparam and NFS always agree;
.Li dump
gets an empty answer when there is no such export, and
.Li gateway
the router and netmask.
Unknown clients and keys get no answer, so that another server can answer.
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Ic server_name No = Ar string
The name announced as the NFS server.
The default is the host name.
.It Ic domain No = Ar string
The NIS domain announced to clients.
The default is the
.Ic nis
service's domain, for hosts that may use
.Ic nis .
.It Ic router No = Ar address
The default is bootd's address on the network.
.El
.It Ic service Qq nfs
NFS version 2 and MOUNT version 1 over UDP.
It serves the host's
.Ic export Ns s ;
a client may only mount its own, and may change only those that are
.Ic writable .
Files are created and written on disk, owned by the user bootd runs as.
What the disk cannot hold is kept in the export's
.Ic spec
instead: owners other than bootd's, device nodes, and modes that would
keep bootd from reading or writing a file.
When bootd runs as root, owners are set on disk, and on Linux device
nodes and fifos too.
A call the client sends again because the answer was lost gets the same
answer, not an error.
Permissions are left to the client, as for its own disk.
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Ic sync No = Ar bool
Write data to disk before answering, as NFS version 2 requires.
The default is false, which is much faster, but writes a client was told
are done can be lost if the server crashes.
.El
.It Ic service Qq nis
NIS (YP), read only, for one domain.
The maps are made from files in
.Pa /etc
format in the
.Ic maps
directory:
.Pa passwd ,
.Pa group ,
.Pa hosts ,
.Pa ethers ,
.Pa netgroup ,
.Pa services ,
.Pa protocols ,
.Pa rpc ,
.Pa networks ,
.Pa netmasks ,
.Pa aliases ,
.Pa publickey
and
.Pa bootparams ,
keyed as SunOS's
.Pa /var/yp/Makefile
keys them.
A missing file means no map.
The configured hosts are added to the
.Pa hosts
and
.Pa ethers
maps, and so is bootd itself, with its address on each network.
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Ic domain No = Ar string
The NIS domain.
Required.
.It Ic maps No = Ar path
The directory with the source files.
.It Ic server_name No = Ar string
The master's name, added to the hosts maps.
The default is the host name up to the first dot.
.El
.It Ic service Qq dhcp
BOOTP and DHCP.
Clients are identified by Ethernet address and get the host's IP address;
there is no address pool.
The reply names bootd as the TFTP server, and the host's first file as the
boot file, or the file matching the name the client asks for.
It also carries the host's name, the subnet mask and broadcast address,
and the host's
.Ic export Qq root
as root path,
.Ar server : Ns Ar path .
Hosts that may use
.Ic nis
also get the NIS domain and bootd as NIS server.
BOOTP clients get these options in RFC 1048 format if their vendor area
has the magic cookie or is empty.
Unknown clients get no answer, and requests that name another server are
left to it, so that bootd can share a network with another DHCP server.
Requests through relay agents are not answered.
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Ic routers No = Ar list
Routers announced to clients on their subnet.
The default is none.
.It Ic dns No = Ar list
Name servers announced to clients.
.It Ic domain No = Ar string
The DNS domain announced to clients.
.It Ic lease_time No = Ar seconds
The default is 86400, one day.
.El
.It Ic service Qq http
HTTP/1.1, read only, for installers that fetch their files by URL, such
as NetBSD's sysinst or a Solaris flash archive.
As with WebNFS, a URL's path is the path an NFS client would mount: an
.Ic export
with
.Ic export_path
.Pa /pub/netbsd
holds
.Pa http://server/pub/netbsd/amd64/binary/sets/ .
The host's
.Ic file Ns s
are at the top, under their names and aliases, but not under a
.Qq *
name.
Directories are listed, including those above the export paths, and
ranges are served.
There are no index pages or CGI programs, so Solaris WAN boot's
.Pa wanboot-cgi
cannot be served.
Clients are identified by IP address; exports with a
.Ic server
are left out.
Symbolic links may not lead out of their export, and an export's
.Ic spec
is not used.
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Ic port No = Ar number
The default is 80.
.El
.It Ic service Qq ftp
Anonymous FTP, read only, with the same files and paths as
.Ic http .
The users
.Qq anonymous
and
.Qq ftp
log in with any password; there are no others.
Passive (PASV, EPSV) and active (PORT, EPRT) data connections work, but
active ones only to the client's own address.
SIZE, MDTM and REST are understood, so interrupted transfers can be
resumed.
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Ic port No = Ar number
The control port.
The default is 21.
.El
.El
.El
.Sh HOSTS AND CLASSES
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Ic host Qo Ar name Qc Brq ...
A client.
Its name is also its host name for bootparam and NIS.
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Ic mac No = Ar address
The Ethernet address.
The default is looked up in the
.Ic ethers
file.
.It Ic ip No = Ar address
The IP address.
The default is looked up by name.
.It Ic class No = Ar name
A class whose services, files, disks and exports the host gets as well.
The host's own take precedence over the class's of the same name.
.It Ic services No = Ar list
The services the host may use.
The default is the class's, or every enabled service;
.Li \&"all\&"
means every enabled service.
.It Ic file , disk , export
See
.Sx FILES ,
below.
.El
.It Ic class Qo Ar name Qc Brq ...
Settings shared by hosts, with
.Ic services ,
.Ic file ,
.Ic disk
and
.Ic export
as in a host.
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Ic match No = Ar map
Serve clients that have no host entry when they identify as the given
model.
The only key is
.Ic rmp_machtype ,
the machine type an HP boot ROM sends, e.g.\&
.Li { rmp_machtype = \&"HPS300\&" } .
.El
.El
.Sh FILES
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Ic file Qo Ar path Qc Brq ...
A file a host may load.
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Ic name No = Ar string
The name the client asks for.
The default is the base name of
.Ar path ;
.Li \&"*\&"
matches any name;
a file sent because only such a name matched is logged as such.
.It Ic aliases No = Ar list
Other names for the file.
.It Ic services No = Ar list
The services that may serve it.
The default is all the host's services.
.It Ic load No = Ar address
For RPL, where the file is loaded, e.g.\&
.Li \&"0x8000\&" .
.It Ic execute No = Ar address
For RPL, where the client starts after loading.
The first file that has it sets it; the default is the first file's load
address.
.It Ic floppy No = Ar bool
For RPL, boot the file as a floppy disk image, without
.Ic load
or
.Ic execute .
.El
.It Ic disk Qo Ar name Qc Brq ...
A disk served over ND.
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Ic path No = Ar path
The disk image, or the first-stage boot program.
Required.
.It Ic mode No = Ar string
.Cm image
(the default), a disk image, or
.Cm bootfile ,
which works like NetBSD's ndbootd: the boot program in
.Ic path
(at most 15 blocks) is served from block 1 on.
A
.Cm bootfile
disk is also served as public unit 0
.Pq Li ndp0 ,
which the Sun-2 PROM boots from.
.It Ic boot2 No = Ar path
For
.Cm bootfile
disks, a second-stage boot program served from block 16 on.
.It Ic partition No = Ar letter
For images, serve one partition
.Pf ( Cm a
to
.Cm h )
of an image with a Sun disk label.
.It Ic offset No = Ar sectors
.It Ic size No = Ar sectors
For images, serve part of the image instead, like
.Pa /etc/nd.local ;
a
.Ic size
of 0, the default, means up to the end.
.It Ic boot No = Ar path
For images, a boot program such as SunOS's
.Pa bootnd
served in place of blocks 1 to 15, where the PROM loads it from.
.It Ic unit No = Ar number
The unit number:
.Li nd Ns Ar n ,
or
.Li ndp Ns Ar n
with
.Ic public .
The default is 0.
.It Ic public No = Ar bool
Serve the disk as public unit
.Li ndp Ns Ar n ,
shared by clients and read only.
.It Ic writable No = Ar bool
Let the client write to the disk, e.g. its root and swap.
The default is false; writes to read-only disks get an error.
.El
.It Ic export Qo Ar name Qc Brq ...
A directory or file served over NFS and announced by bootparam, usually
.Qq root
and
.Qq swap ,
and served over HTTP and FTP at its
.Ic export_path .
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Ic path No = Ar path
Required.
.It Ic server No = Ar string
Another NFS server that serves the export, which bootparam announces
instead of bootd.
.It Ic spec No = Ar path
An
.Xr mtree 5
specification of the export's files.
The owners, modes, device nodes and symbolic links it lists are served as
given, so that a root file system extracted without root privileges can
be served, e.g. with a specification made by
.Dl tar -cf root.mtree --format=mtree @root.tgz
The default is
.Ar path Ns Pa .mtree
next to a directory, if it exists or the export is
.Ic writable .
A writable export saves what clients set there, a second after they set
it, in the full path form and without the keywords bootd does not use,
such as checksums and sizes; the file and its directory must be
writable by bootd.
.It Ic export_path No = Ar path
The path clients mount and bootparam announces.
The default is the absolute
.Ic path .
NetBSD keeps
.Ar server : Ns Ar path
in 90 bytes.
.It Ic writable No = Ar bool
Let the client create, change and remove files in the export, e.g. its
root, or write to it if it is a file, e.g. its swap.
The default is false.
.El
.El
.Sh EXAMPLES
A SPARCstation that loads its boot program over TFTP and mounts its root
over NFS, attached through
.Xr bootbridge 8 :
.Bd -literal -offset indent
root = "/srv/netboot"

network "lab" {
  address = "192.168.1.250/24"
  udp     = "127.0.0.1:4712"
  peers   = ["127.0.0.1:4711"]
}

service "rarp" {}
service "tftp" {}
service "bootparam" {}
service "nfs" {}

host "kali" {
  # mac and ip are looked up in /etc/ethers and DNS.
  services = ["rarp", "tftp", "bootparam", "nfs"]

  # The boot PROM asks for its IP address in hex.
  file "files/sparc/solaris-2.5.1-sun4c" { name = "*" }
  export "root" { path = "/export/hosts/kali/root" }
  export "swap" { path = "/export/hosts/kali/swap" }
}
.Ed
.Pp
HP 9000/300 machines without host entries, chosen by machine type:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
service "rmp" {}

class "hp300" {
  match    = { rmp_machtype = "HPS300" }
  services = ["rmp"]

  file "files/hp300/netbsd-uboot" { name = "netbsd" }
}
.Ed
.Pp
A Sun-2 booting NetBSD over ND:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
host "sun2" {
  services = ["rarp", "nd", "bootparam", "nfs"]

  disk "boot" {
    path  = "files/sun2/bootyy"
    boot2 = "files/sun2/netboot"
    mode  = "bootfile"
  }
  export "root" {
    path = "/export/hosts/sun2/root"
    spec = "/export/hosts/sun2/root.mtree"
  }
}
.Ed
.Pp
A NetBSD install server: sysinst fetches the sets from
.Pa http://192.168.1.250/pub/netbsd-10.1/amd64/binary/sets/
or over FTP from the same path, and the miniroot kernel is at
.Pa /netbsd-INSTALL.gz :
.Bd -literal -offset indent
service "http" {}
service "ftp" {}

host "newbox" {
  mac      = "52:54:00:12:34:56"
  ip       = "192.168.1.20"
  services = ["dhcp", "tftp", "http", "ftp"]
  file "netbsd/pxeboot_ia32.bin" {}
  file "netbsd/netbsd-INSTALL.gz" {}
  export "netbsd" {
    path        = "/srv/netbsd-10.1"
    export_path = "/pub/netbsd-10.1"
  }
}
.Ed
.Pp
A network reached over L2TPv3 from
.Xr bootbridge 8
on another machine:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
network "remote" {
  address = "192.168.1.251/24"
  udp     = "0.0.0.0:1701"
  peers   = ["192.168.1.10:1701"]
  l2tp { cookie = "0x0123456789abcdef" }
}
.Ed
.Pp
The packages include a commented example with every setting, in
.Pa /usr/share/doc/gobootd/examples/bootd.hcl
on Debian and Ubuntu.
